# Declared lint toolchain — the human-edited source of truth for the versions
# CI's `lint` job and local `make lint` both install.
#
# EDIT THIS FILE, then run `python3 scripts/lock_toolchain.py` to regenerate the
# hash-pinned `lint-tools.txt` beside it. CI installs the .txt (with pip
# enforcing every hash), never this file; `make lint` fails on drift.
#
# Resolved for Python 3.12 — the version CI's lint job pins.
#
# Why pin at all: these tools were once installed UNPINNED (`pip install ruff mypy …`), so
# every CI run pulled the latest release. When ruff 0.16.0 shipped it began
# flagging rule families this repo deliberately does not select (UP/BLE/PLW —
# ~1100 findings of exactly the pyupgrade-style churn the roadmap already
# declined), turning the lint gate red on every open PR and on main, with no
# code change. A lint gate whose meaning can shift out from under you on an
# upstream release is not a gate. Pinning here freezes the ruleset to a known
# state; upgrades become a deliberate, reviewable bump of this file.
ruff==0.16.0
mypy==2.3.0
codespell==2.4.3
import-linter==2.13
vulture==2.16
xenon==0.9.3
pyright==1.1.411
interrogate==1.7.0
# refurb: modernization smells. ruff ports only a handful of the FURB checks
# (FURB167 alone fires here); refurb's own mypy-backed analysis found 70 in
# boost_cli — contextlib.suppress candidates, redundant list()/dict()/str()
# casts, `x or ""` on a value that is already non-optional. It needs >=3.10
# itself, which is why this set is lint-only and never a runtime dependency.
# Reuses the mypy pinned above.
refurb==2.3.1
# shellcheck, as a pinned dependency rather than an assumption. actionlint does
# not lint `run:` blocks itself — it shells out to shellcheck, and when
# shellcheck is not on PATH it skips that entirely and still exits 0. Same
# workflow file, same actionlint: without shellcheck, no output and exit 0; with
# it, `SC2001` and exit 1. CI got away with it because the GitHub runner image
# happens to preinstall shellcheck, which is an unpinned, unversioned, silently
# revocable dependency on somebody else's base image — and locally, where it is
# usually absent, `make lint` was passing a check it never ran. Pinning it here
# puts it on both sides and makes the version reviewable like every other tool.
shellcheck-py==0.11.0.1
